+ column  Message

- column  Resolution
- row  `↯/s.100.2.1`  `Ipv4ByteOutOfRange`  An IPv4 byte was numeric but outside the range 0–255.  `[↯/s.100.2.1] the IP address is not valid because the number 300 is not in the range 0-255`  Use only values 0–255 for each IPv4 byte.
- row  `↯/s.100.2.2`  `Ipv4ByteNotNumeric`  An IPv4 component was not a number.  `[↯/s.100.2.2] the IP address is not valid because the part abc is not a number`  Use four decimal numbers separated by `.`.
- row  `↯/s.100.2.3`  `Ipv4WrongNumberOfGroups`  The address did not have exactly four `.`-separated groups.  `[↯/s.100.2.3] the IP address is not valid because the address contains 3 period-separated groups instead of 4`  Provide exactly four numeric groups.
- row  `↯/s.100.2.4`  `Ipv6GroupWrongLength`  An IPv6 group is more than four hex characters long.  `[↯/s.100.2.4] the IP address is not valid because the group is more than 4 hexadecimal characters long`  Each IPv6 group must be 1–4 hex characters; trim leading zeroes if necessary.
- row  `↯/s.100.2.5`  `Ipv6GroupNotHex`  An IPv6 group contains a non-hexadecimal character.  `[↯/s.100.2.5] the IP address is not valid because the group 'xyz1' is not a hexadecimal number`  Use only `0`-`9` and `a`-`f`/`A`-`F` in IPv6 groups.
- row  `↯/s.100.2.6`  `Ipv6TooManyNonzeroGroups`  The address has more non-zero groups than 8 — indicating a malformed input.  `[↯/s.100.2.6] the IP address is not valid because the address has 9 non-zero groups, which is more than is permitted`  IPv6 has at most 8 groups; check the input.
- row  `↯/s.100.2.7`  `Ipv6WrongNumberOfGroups`  After expansion of `::`, the address does not have exactly 8 groups.  `[↯/s.100.2.7] the IP address is not valid because the address has 7 groups, but should have 8`  Add the missing group, or use `::` to compress consecutive zero groups.
- row  `↯/s.100.2.8`  `Ipv6MultipleDoubleColons`  The address contains `::` more than once, which would be ambiguous.  `[↯/s.100.2.8] the IP address is not valid because :: appears more than once`  Use `::` at most once per address; expand any extras to explicit `0` groups.
- row  `↯/s.100.2.9`  `Ipv4SubnetPrefixOutOfRange`  An IPv4 subnet's prefix length is outside the range a 32-bit address permits.  `[↯/s.100.2.9] the prefix length 40 is not in the range 0-32`  Use a prefix length between 0 and 32.
- row  `↯/s.100.2.10`  `Ipv6SubnetPrefixOutOfRange`  An IPv6 subnet's prefix length is outside the range a 128-bit address permits.  `[↯/s.100.2.10] the prefix length 200 is not in the range 0-128`  Use a prefix length between 0 and 128.
- row  `↯/s.100.2.11`  `SubnetPrefixNotNumeric`  The text after the `/` in a subnet is not a number.  `[↯/s.100.2.11] the prefix length ab is not a number`  Write the prefix length in decimal, as in `192.168.0.0/24`.
- row  `↯/s.100.2.12`  `SubnetWrongFormat`  A subnet is not a single address and prefix separated by one `/`.  `[↯/s.100.2.12] the subnet contains 3 slash-separated parts instead of 2`  Write the subnet as `address/prefix`, with exactly one slash.
- row  `↯/s.100.2.13`  `Ipv4WrongNumberOfBytes`  Data decoded as an `Ipv4` (`data.as[Ipv4]`) is not exactly four bytes long.  `[↯/s.100.2.13] the address is 16 bytes long instead of 4`  Decode sixteen-byte data as an `Ipv6`; check the length before choosing, as `java.net.InetAddress#getAddress` yields either.
- row  `↯/s.100.2.14`  `Ipv6WrongNumberOfBytes`  Data decoded as an `Ipv6` (`data.as[Ipv6]`) is not exactly sixteen bytes long.  `[↯/s.100.2.14] the address is 4 bytes long instead of 16`  Decode four-byte data as an `Ipv4`.
+ column  Résolution
+ row  `↯/s.100.2.1`  `Ipv4ByteOutOfRange`  Un octet IPv4 était numérique, mais hors de l'intervalle 0–255.  `[↯/s.100.2.1] the IP address is not valid because the number 300 is not in the range 0-255`  N'utilisez que des valeurs de 0 à 255 pour chaque octet IPv4.
+ row  `↯/s.100.2.2`  `Ipv4ByteNotNumeric`  Une composante IPv4 n'était pas un nombre.  `[↯/s.100.2.2] the IP address is not valid because the part abc is not a number`  Utilisez quatre nombres décimaux séparés par `.`.
+ row  `↯/s.100.2.3`  `Ipv4WrongNumberOfGroups`  L'adresse n'avait pas exactement quatre groupes séparés par `.`.  `[↯/s.100.2.3] the IP address is not valid because the address contains 3 period-separated groups instead of 4`  Fournissez exactement quatre groupes numériques.
+ row  `↯/s.100.2.4`  `Ipv6GroupWrongLength`  Un groupe IPv6 compte plus de quatre caractères hexadécimaux.  `[↯/s.100.2.4] the IP address is not valid because the group is more than 4 hexadecimal characters long`  Chaque groupe IPv6 doit compter de 1 à 4 caractères hexadécimaux ; supprimez les zéros initiaux si nécessaire.
+ row  `↯/s.100.2.5`  `Ipv6GroupNotHex`  Un groupe IPv6 contient un caractère non hexadécimal.  `[↯/s.100.2.5] the IP address is not valid because the group 'xyz1' is not a hexadecimal number`  N'utilisez que `0`-`9` et `a`-`f`/`A`-`F` dans les groupes IPv6.
+ row  `↯/s.100.2.6`  `Ipv6TooManyNonzeroGroups`  L'adresse a plus de 8 groupes non nuls — signe d'une entrée malformée.  `[↯/s.100.2.6] the IP address is not valid because the address has 9 non-zero groups, which is more than is permitted`  IPv6 a au plus 8 groupes ; vérifiez l'entrée.
+ row  `↯/s.100.2.7`  `Ipv6WrongNumberOfGroups`  Après expansion de `::`, l'adresse n'a pas exactement 8 groupes.  `[↯/s.100.2.7] the IP address is not valid because the address has 7 groups, but should have 8`  Ajoutez le groupe manquant, ou utilisez `::` pour compresser des groupes nuls consécutifs.
+ row  `↯/s.100.2.8`  `Ipv6MultipleDoubleColons`  L'adresse contient `::` plus d'une fois, ce qui serait ambigu.  `[↯/s.100.2.8] the IP address is not valid because :: appears more than once`  N'utilisez `::` qu'une fois au plus par adresse ; développez les autres en groupes `0` explicites.
+ row  `↯/s.100.2.9`  `Ipv4SubnetPrefixOutOfRange`  La longueur de préfixe d'un sous-réseau IPv4 est hors de l'intervalle que permet une adresse de 32 bits.  `[↯/s.100.2.9] the prefix length 40 is not in the range 0-32`  Utilisez une longueur de préfixe comprise entre 0 et 32.
+ row  `↯/s.100.2.10`  `Ipv6SubnetPrefixOutOfRange`  La longueur de préfixe d'un sous-réseau IPv6 est hors de l'intervalle que permet une adresse de 128 bits.  `[↯/s.100.2.10] the prefix length 200 is not in the range 0-128`  Utilisez une longueur de préfixe comprise entre 0 et 128.
+ row  `↯/s.100.2.11`  `SubnetPrefixNotNumeric`  Le texte qui suit la `/` d'un sous-réseau n'est pas un nombre.  `[↯/s.100.2.11] the prefix length ab is not a number`  Écrivez la longueur de préfixe en décimal, comme dans `192.168.0.0/24`.
+ row  `↯/s.100.2.12`  `SubnetWrongFormat`  Un sous-réseau n'est pas formé d'une seule adresse et d'un préfixe séparés par une unique `/`.  `[↯/s.100.2.12] the subnet contains 3 slash-separated parts instead of 2`  Écrivez le sous-réseau sous la forme `address/prefix`, avec exactement une barre oblique.
+ row  `↯/s.100.2.13`  `Ipv4WrongNumberOfBytes`  Des données décodées comme `Ipv4` (`data.as[Ipv4]`) ne font pas exactement quatre octets.  `[↯/s.100.2.13] the address is 16 bytes long instead of 4`  Décodez des données de seize octets comme `Ipv6` ; vérifiez la longueur avant de choisir, car `java.net.InetAddress#getAddress` produit l'une ou l'autre.
+ row  `↯/s.100.2.14`  `Ipv6WrongNumberOfBytes`  Des données décodées comme `Ipv6` (`data.as[Ipv6]`) ne font pas exactement seize octets.  `[↯/s.100.2.14] the address is 4 bytes long instead of 16`  Décodez des données de quatre octets comme `Ipv4`.

